    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "E BROWN lE DEt\nDIMENSIONS:\nLENGTH OF XEST.\nLENGTH ON DECK\nLENGTH OVERALL\n(from kniaht\nhenda to tattrasi\nFEN\nadored according to and of Congres in the your 1859 6y N. Cures in the Clash Offer of the Dutie Court of Now Your\nDonald Mo Kay eog, builder of the Magnificent\nCLIPPER SHIP \"SOVEREIGN OF THE SEAS\".\nThis print is roopoctfully dodicated by the Publisher.\nLITH RY N. CURRIER\nFEET\n23½ FEE\n2421",
      "Pan Am.\nSherry\nDubonnet\nCampari\nNoilly Prat\nManhattan\nGin Martini\nVodka Martini\nBloody Mary\nScrewdriver\nWhiskey Sour\nTom Collins\nVodka Collins\nScotch\nBourbon\nCanadian\nGin\nVodka\nRum\nBeer\nWines of Burgundy\nWines of Bordeaux\n—from the wine cellars of our Inter-Continental Hotels\nChampagne\nWines of Australia\nContreau\nDrambuie\nBénédictine Creme de Menthe\nCognac VSOP\nBénédictine and Brandy\n523 JA",
      "HORS D'OEUVRE\nSpecialties from Various Countries\nENTREES\nGrilled Filet Mignon Maitre D'Hôtel\nBreast of Chicken in Curry Sauce\nSalmon Braised in Champagne Sauce\nButtered Garden Vegetables\nPilaff Rice\nriti\nGreen Salad\nA Selection of Cheese\n5-X71\nDessert\n#Huh\nCoffee\n.-.-\nSanka\nTea\n523 JA CC (3350) 6/73",
      "CLIPPER SHIP \"SOVEREIGN OF THE SEAS\"\nThe front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship print.\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipper\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduced\nthrough the courtesy of The Museum of the City of New York.\nThe clipper ship \"Sovereign of the Seas,\" launched in 1852, influenced the design of all future\nclippers. She was built with almost a flat bottomed hull to allow for greater cargo carrying capacity,\nwhile maintaining speed. Many of the world's ship builders claimed that this was impossible to\nachieve. On her maiden run to San Francisco, with a mainmast that reached over 200 feet into the\nsky, and able to carry more than 12,000 yards of canvas, she made San Francisco in 103 days. To\nreport such record speeds was considered fantastic and incredible. It convinced the most skeptical\nof the value of flat bottoms for speed. On her maiden voyage, one of the passengers wrote in his\ndiary, \"It was fearful to see the topmasts bend as she beat around the Horn. We hardly dared look\naloft lest we should see the canvas blown away.\" To cap it off she then broke the record from\nNew York to Liverpool, making the run in 13 days, 13½ hours. \"Sovereign of the Seas\" reported\nthe highest rate of speed ever achieved by a sailing ship-\n-22 knots. The fastest of today's ocean\nliners steam along at about 31.5 knots.\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers* of the air, the early clipper ships traveled the lanes of com-\nmerce to all the far places of the world... to Japan... to Australia... and, in the days of the great\ngold rush, from America's east coast, around Cape Horn, South America, to California. Our current\nequipment.. the Clipper fleet of the Twentieth Century... consists of the finest commercial\naircraft ever developed-aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record for safety,\nspeed and service.\n*CLIPPER TRADE MARK-REG US PAT OFFICE-COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMERICAN WORLD AIRWAYS INC\nLIHO IN U.S.A.-8739-0393\n2016.088.016"
    ],
